  "body": {
    "id": "prop:single-hazard-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "In the single-informative-hazard graph, \\(\\mathsf A_1\\) terminates after \\(k(T-j+1)\\) checks and \\(\\mathsf C_1=1\\) exactly when some \\(d<k\\) audits some \\(X_\\ell\\) with \\(\\ell\\ge j\\). The successful branch uses the explicit audited-marginal or risk-set reconstruction, the failure branch uses the explicit positive scaling witness, and the minimal designs are exactly the singleton incidences.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "T",
      "t",
      "d",
      "j",
      "k",
      "ell",
      "S",
      "B",
      "Crit"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:substantive-markov",
      "ass:substantive-positivity",
      "ass:hazard-positivity",
      "def:criterion",
      "thm:constructive-completeness",
      "thm:single-hazard-alternative",
      "thm:minimal-design-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "The proposition packages the incidence check, recovery, witness, and frontier as a directly usable arbitrary-horizon rule.",
    "gap": "Passive self-censoring theory does not say which downstream state measured in which dropout stratum destroys the cancellation.",
    "consumer": "A scientist who can isolate one informative hazard obtains a closed-form measurement decision.",
    "proof_tex": "The displayed incidence condition is exactly \\(\\mathcal H(B)\\ne\\varnothing\\).  The scan length and the two constructive outputs follow from \\(\\text{thm:single-hazard-alternative}\\), while\n\\[\n\\mathsf C_1=1\\Longleftrightarrow\\mathcal H(B)\\ne\\varnothing\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\operatorname{GID}(T,S,B)=1\n\\]\nis \\(\\text{thm:constructive-completeness}\\).  Finally, \\(\\text{thm:minimal-design-frontier}\\) proves that a minimal success contains exactly one such incidence and no extraneous audited coordinate.  These three cited results supply respectively the explicit reconstruction or witness, the necessary-and-sufficient causal conclusion, and the exact frontier.",
